From: Brad Barnett <127794626+bdbarnett@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 22:28:10 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] sdio_drv: drop received packets when there's no buffer for
 them, instead of writing them to NULL.

sdio_push_data_to_queue takes a receive buffer for each packet in the
stream from sdio_buffer_alloc, which comes from internal, DMA-capable
memory, and only asserts the result. With asserts compiled out, when that
memory runs out the packet is copied to address 0 and the board resets
(Store access fault in memcpy, MTVAL 0, in sdio_data_to_rx_buf_task). The
transmit side already checks after its assert. Now the receive side drops
the rest of the stream, as it does for a stream it can't decode, and the
network stack sees lost frames instead.
---
 host/drivers/transport/sdio/sdio_drv.c | 7 +++++++
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)

diff --git a/host/drivers/transport/sdio/sdio_drv.c b/host/drivers/transport/sdio/sdio_drv.c
index a5ade7c..b2557db 100644
--- a/host/drivers/transport/sdio/sdio_drv.c
+++ b/host/drivers/transport/sdio/sdio_drv.c
@@ -860,6 +860,13 @@ static esp_err_t sdio_push_data_to_queue(uint8_t * buf, uint32_t buf_len)
 		/* Allocate rx buffer */
 		pkt_rxbuff = sdio_buffer_alloc(MEMSET_REQUIRED);
 		assert(pkt_rxbuff);
+		if (unlikely(!pkt_rxbuff)) {
+			/* No DMA-capable memory for a receive buffer: drop the rest
+			 * of the stream, as a stream that can't be decoded is
+			 * dropped above, instead of copying it to NULL. */
+			ESP_LOGE(TAG, "No rx buffer: dropping packet(s) from stream");
+			return ESP_FAIL;
+		}
 
 		packet_size = len + offset;
 		if (packet_size > buf_len) {
-- 
2.43.0

